#
# THE BUILD CONTEXT'S OWN .dockerignore.
#
# pwa/ is built as its OWN docker context (`context: ./pwa` in
# docker-compose.yml), and pwa/Dockerfile.prod does `COPY . .`. A build
# context uses the .dockerignore in ITS OWN ROOT, so the root .dockerignore
# does NOT apply to this image -- and the root rule set is the branch's rule
# set, translated to this context.
#
# WHY IT MATTERS: the image is built from the Docker build CONTEXT while the
# AGPL-3.0 section 13 offer publishes the COMMITTED tree. Anything git
# ignores is invisible to `git status --porcelain` -- so the deploy stamp's
# dirty check passes -- but it is still copied into the image. That would make
# the offer's promise ("the archive is the exact tree of the running build")
# false.
#
# THIS IS NOT HYPOTHETICAL FOR PWA: vite reads .env files at BUILD
# time and INLINES their values into the shipped bundle, so a developer-local
# pwa/.env.local would ship to users while being absent from the published
# archive.
#
# The rule is pinned by tests/test_dockerignore_build_contexts.py and defended
# by a mutation spec.
#
.git
.gitignore
**/.gitignore
Dockerfile
Dockerfile.*
**/Dockerfile*
docker-compose.yml
.env
.env.*
*.env
**/*.env
**/.env
**/.env.*
!.env.example
gcp_key.json
**/gcp_key.json
*.key
**/*.key
*.pem
**/*.pem
*.p12
**/*.p12
*.pfx
**/*.pfx
*.crt
**/*.crt
*.cer
**/*.cer
*.secret
**/*.secret
*.token
**/*.token
